  16:     Shift each object pixel one step right unless it is on the object's lower-right frontier.
  17: 
  18:     Input structure:
  19:     - The input contains one or more same-color objects on a black background.
  20:     - Objects are connected components and may have diagonal or slanted edges.
  21:     - Each object is processed independently in its original position.
  22: 
  23:     Rule:
  24:     - For every non-black pixel in an object, look for any other object pixel down and to the right.
  25:     - If such a pixel exists, move the current pixel one column to the right.
  26:     - If not, leave the pixel in place.
  27: 
  28:     Key insight:
  29:     - The local move is controlled by the object's southeast support: pixels with something beyond
  30:       them down-right slide, while the terminal southeast contour stays fixed.
  31:     """
  32:     # find the connected components, which are monochromatic objects
  33:     objects = find_components(input_grid, bg=Color.BLACK, connectivity=8, monochromatic=True, return_format="objects")
  34: 
  35:     output_grid = np.zeros_like(input_grid)
  36: 
  37:     for obj in objects:
  38:         transformed_object = np.zeros_like(obj)
  39: 
  40:         for r in range(obj.shape[0]):
  41:             for c in range(obj.shape[1]):
  42:                 if obj[r, c] != Color.BLACK:
  43:                     # check that there are other colored pixels down and to the right
  44:                     down_and_to_the_right = obj[r+1:, c+1:]
  45:                     if np.any(down_and_to_the_right != Color.BLACK):
  46:                         transformed_object[r, c+1] = obj[r, c]
  47:                     else:
  48:                         transformed_object[r, c] = obj[r, c]
  49: 
  50:         paste(output_grid, transformed_object, 0, 0, transparent=Color.BLACK, clip=True)
  51: 
  52:     return output_grid
  53: 
